1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,110
Two travel websites. Two separate origins. One assistant
that can cross between them, but only with your permission.

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:18,940
Start on Skyline Air. Pick a flight, enter a passenger,
and book it. The page holds the state, issues the reference,
and never leaves the browser.

3
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:47,160
Now ask the concierge about a hotel. The model wants to call
Harbor Hotel, a different origin published by a different site.
This take uses a scripted model endpoint, so the handoff is
reproducible. The same page runs Otari with a key. Before
anything leaves the page, you see who will be contacted, why,
and the exact request.

4
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:56,160
Decline. The runtime stops the call. Zero requests reach
the hotel, and the assistant tells you so.

5
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:11,870
Ask again, and allow. The runtime fetches the hotel's published
manifest, checks its policy, and runs its network. Harbor Hotel
receives the request, not your chat history, and not your model key.

6
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:31,990
The trace shows every hop. Origin, peer, consent, and result.
Fourteen tests cover the runtime and the boundary.

7
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:45,200
Trip Relay. Two sites, one visible boundary. Live on GitHub Pages.
MIT licensed. Built on the MVP Profile, with Mozilla Otari as the
default router.
